[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Collections Utility Class: Sorting, Searching, Synchronization"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `java.util.Collections` class is a utility class that provides static methods for operating on or returning collections. It offers a variety of functionalities, including sorting, searching, synchronization, and more. This tutorial will focus on sorting, searching, and synchronization using the `Collections` class."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"Sorting Collections"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `Collections.sort()` method is used to sort a list. The list elements must implement the `Comparable` interface, or you must provide a `Comparator` to define the sorting order."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Sorting Lists of Comparable Objects"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"If the elements in the list implement the `Comparable` interface, you can directly use `Collections.sort()` to sort the list in ascending order (natural ordering)."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.List;

public class SortComparableExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Integer> numbers = new ArrayList<>();
        numbers.add(5);
        numbers.add(1);
        numbers.add(3);
        numbers.add(2);
        numbers.add(4);

        System.out.println("Before sorting: " + numbers);
        Collections.sort(numbers);
        System.out.println("After sorting: " + numbers);
    }
}"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the above example, the `Integer` class implements the `Comparable` interface, so `Collections.sort()` sorts the list of integers in ascending order."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Sorting Lists with a Comparator"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"If you need to sort a list based on a custom ordering or if the elements don't implement `Comparable`, you can provide a `Comparator` to the `Collections.sort()` method."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.Comparator;
import java.util.List;

class Employee {
    String name;
    int age;

    public Employee(String name, int age) {
        this.name = name;
        this.age = age;
    }

    public String getName() {
        return name;
    }

    public int getAge() {
        return age;
    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return "Employee{" +
                "name='" + name + '\'' +
                ", age=" + age +
                '}';
    }
}

public class SortComparatorExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Employee> employees = new ArrayList<>();
        employees.add(new Employee("Alice", 30));
        employees.add(new Employee("Bob", 25));
        employees.add(new Employee("Charlie", 35));

        System.out.println("Before sorting: " + employees);

        // Sort by age using a Comparator
        Collections.sort(employees, Comparator.comparingInt(Employee::getAge));

        System.out.println("After sorting by age: " + employees);

        // Sort by name using a Comparator
        Collections.sort(employees, Comparator.comparing(Employee::getName));

        System.out.println("After sorting by name: " + employees);
    }
}"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, we create a custom `Employee` class and sort a list of `Employee` objects first by age and then by name using `Comparator.comparingInt()` and `Comparator.comparing()` respectively."},{"order":13,"type":"heading","text":"Searching Collections"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `Collections.binarySearch()` method is used to search for an element in a sorted list. It uses the binary search algorithm, which is very efficient for searching in sorted data. The list *must* be sorted before using `binarySearch()`."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Searching Lists of Comparable Objects"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"If the list elements implement `Comparable`, you can use `Collections.binarySearch()` directly. It returns the index of the element if found, or a negative value if not found. The negative value represents `(-(insertion point) - 1)`, where the insertion point is the point at which the key would be inserted into the list."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.List;

public class BinarySearchComparableExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Integer> numbers = new ArrayList<>();
        numbers.add(1);
        numbers.add(2);
        numbers.add(3);
        numbers.add(4);
        numbers.add(5);

        int index = Collections.binarySearch(numbers, 3);

        if (index >= 0) {
            System.out.println("Element found at index: " + index);
        } else {
            System.out.println("Element not found");
        }
    }
}"},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Searching Lists with a Comparator"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"If the list was sorted using a `Comparator`, you must provide the same `Comparator` to `Collections.binarySearch()` to ensure correct results."},{"order":20,"type":"code","text":"import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.Comparator;
import java.util.List;

class Employee {
    String name;
    int age;

    public Employee(String name, int age) {
        this.name = name;
        this.age = age;
    }

    public String getName() {
        return name;
    }

    public int getAge() {
        return age;
    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return "Employee{" +
                "name='" + name + '\'' +
                ", age=" + age +
                '}';
    }
}

public class BinarySearchComparatorExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Employee> employees = new ArrayList<>();
        employees.add(new Employee("Alice", 30));
        employees.add(new Employee("Bob", 25));
        employees.add(new Employee("Charlie", 35));

        // Sort by age
        Comparator<Employee> ageComparator = Comparator.comparingInt(Employee::getAge);
        Collections.sort(employees, ageComparator);

        Employee searchEmployee = new Employee("David", 25);
        int index = Collections.binarySearch(employees, searchEmployee, ageComparator);

        if (index >= 0) {
            System.out.println("Element found at index: " + index);
        } else {
            System.out.println("Element not found");
        }
    }
}"},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, we sort the `Employee` list by age and then use `binarySearch()` with the same `Comparator` to search for an employee with a specific age. Note that the `searchEmployee` object's name is irrelevant for the search, as we are comparing only based on age."},{"order":22,"type":"heading","text":"Synchronizing Collections"},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `Collections` class provides methods to create synchronized (thread-safe) versions of collections. These methods are useful when multiple threads need to access and modify a collection concurrently."},{"order":24,"type":"subheading","text":"Synchronized List"},{"order":25,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `Collections.synchronizedList()` method returns a synchronized (thread-safe) list backed by the specified list."},{"order":26,"type":"code","text":"import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.List;

public class SynchronizedListExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Integer> numbers = new ArrayList<>();
        List<Integer> synchronizedNumbers = Collections.synchronizedList(numbers);

        // Now, synchronizedNumbers is thread-safe
        // Access and modify synchronizedNumbers using proper synchronization techniques
        // to avoid race conditions.
    }
}"},{"order":27,"type":"paragraph","text":"It's important to note that while `synchronizedList()` makes the list thread-safe, you still need to use proper synchronization techniques (e.g., synchronized blocks or locks) when iterating over the list to avoid `ConcurrentModificationException` or other race conditions."},{"order":28,"type":"subheading","text":"Synchronized Set"},{"order":29,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `Collections.synchronizedSet()` method returns a synchronized (thread-safe) set backed by the specified set."},{"order":30,"type":"code","text":"import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.Set;

public class SynchronizedSetExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Set<String> names = new HashSet<>();
        Set<String> synchronizedNames = Collections.synchronizedSet(names);

        // Now, synchronizedNames is thread-safe
        // Access and modify synchronizedNames using proper synchronization techniques
        // to avoid race conditions.
    }
}"},{"order":31,"type":"subheading","text":"Synchronized Map"},{"order":32,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `Collections.synchronizedMap()` method returns a synchronized (thread-safe) map backed by the specified map."},{"order":33,"type":"code","text":"import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class SynchronizedMapExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Map<String, Integer> ages = new HashMap<>();
        Map<String, Integer> synchronizedAges = Collections.synchronizedMap(ages);

        // Now, synchronizedAges is thread-safe
        // Access and modify synchronizedAges using proper synchronization techniques
        // to avoid race conditions.
    }
}"},{"order":34,"type":"paragraph","text":"Similar to synchronized lists, you need to use explicit synchronization when iterating over the keys or values of a synchronized map."},{"order":35,"type":"paragraph","text":"In summary, the `Collections` utility class provides powerful tools for sorting, searching, and synchronizing collections in Java. Understanding how to use these methods effectively can greatly improve the efficiency and thread-safety of your code."}]
